<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n105040" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τρίμετρος</span></b> 
		<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">[ῐ]</span>, <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ον</span>, of verses, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>consisting of three</i>
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μέτρα;</span> i. e. in iambics, trochaics,
			and anapaestics, of three <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">συζυγίαι</span>
			(of two feet each); but in dactylics, etc., of three single feet:
			hence the iambic verse of six feet is called by the Greeks <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἴαμβος τρίμετρος,</span>
			<i><span class="en">
				Hdt.
				1.12
			</span></i>; so <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τόνος τ.</span>
			<i>trimeter</i> verse, ib.<i><span class="en">
				174
			</span></i>; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἔπη τ.</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Pl.
					<u>Lg.</u>
					810e
				</span></i>
			; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τρίμετρον, τό,</span>
			<i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Nu.</u>
				642
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Arist.
				<u>Po.</u>
				1447b11
			</span></i> (pl.). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b>
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τρίμετρος, ὁ,</span> a measure of capacity for oil, <i><span class="en">
				<u>IG</u>
				14.422
			</span></i><i><span class="en">
				 iii 44
			</span></i> (<span class="entity">Tauromenium</span>).</div>
